Jonathan Shrier
Acting Representative to the UN Economic and Social Council
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President.
In accordance with rules of this body, the United States presents now explanations of our votes on the two resolutions being considered under the current agenda item.
First, concerning resolution A/79/L.95, regarding Wiphala, the United States supports the rights of Indigenous Peoples. We recognize the importance of cultural heritage, traditional knowledge, and cultural expressions.
The Trump Administration has worked to reconnect Native American Tribes and the Native Hawaiian Community with their cultural items, which facilitates the freedoms of expression and religion or belief and ensures their Indigenous heritage is appropriately honored and preserved.
The United States is committed to strengthening Tribal sovereignty, promoting economic opportunities, and enhancing the quality of life for Indigenous Peoples.
Given our steadfast support of Indigenous Peoples and their cultures, we are disappointed to vote no today.
The present draft resolution, A/79/L.95 on the Wiphala, focuses on one specific symbol: one that is limited to a specific sub-region.
This assembly is not in a position to endorse a symbol whose full meaning and purpose continues to be discussed and debated, with no clear agreement, even among the communities that some claim it represents.
We look forward to future resolutions that represent the wide range of Indigenous Peoples, their cultures, traditions, practices, and knowledge systems.
Turning now to the resolution A/79/L.98 on countering hate speech, the United States strongly condemns acts of hate and continues to underscore the harms they can cause to individuals.
Twenty-seven years ago, the United States signed the International Religious Freedom Act into law. Religious freedom is a bedrock American belief.
Regarding the resolution being considered today, although we are voting no, we appreciate Morocco’s leadership and recognize their efforts on this topic. We continue to have concerns that the resolution does not represent the range of views in this body on countering hate speech in line with respect for human rights.
With regard to OP2, we note that the term “hate speech” is too broad and generally encompasses a wide breadth of speech, most of which is protected by the right to freedom of expression, and by freedom of speech in the United States. The United States is concerned that this broad interpretation could be used by governments to silence dissent and crack down on political opponents or citizens who simply have opposing views or politics. In some instances, we have already seen citizens prosecuted for simply sharing political opinions on social media. Any effort to counter hate speech must be carried out in a manner consistent with respect for human rights, including freedom of expression.
Thank you.
