Acting Representative to the UN Economic and Social Council
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President.
The United States also thanks Germany and Gabon for their leadership to counter wildlife trafficking.
The United States is committed to protecting Americans and combatting trafficking in wildlife that profits transnational criminal organizations, and reminds members of their commitments to this same end.
However, the United States is unable to support the present draft resolution, A/79/L.96, “Tackling illicit trafficking in wildlife,” for a number of reasons. First, this resolution contains certain matters that should be discussed in Vienna-based anti-crime fora, rather than in the General Assembly. This subject matter will be discussed by technical experts in Vienna at the Crimes that Affect the Environment Intergovernmental Expert Group Meeting later this month. We do not support efforts to prejudge or amplify policy prescriptions in New York that have not yet been discussed in depth by technical experts.
Furthermore, the United States does not support the process of renewing lengthy resolutions or copying and pasting text from previous years that do not reflect current policy positions. To that end, this resolution contains numerous policy issues the United States does not support.
As previously stated, the United States considers the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and the Sustainable Development Goals a form of soft global governance. The United States will no longer reaffirm them as a matter of course. These issues are beyond the scope of this topic and a distraction to the more pressing issue of advancing improved efforts to disrupt transnational organized crime.
The United States also opposes the use of the term “gender mainstreaming.” It is the policy of the United States to acknowledge the biological reality of sex. Clear and accurate language is necessary to promote the rights and empowerment of women and girls.
The United States finds various other elements of the resolution beyond the scope of countering transnational organized crime, including references to “declining biodiversity,” “sustainable or alternative livelihoods,” “equitable development,” “poverty eradication,” “addressing so-called root causes of trafficking,” “so-called local communities,” “conservation efforts,” “sustainable tourism,” “development cooperation,” and other themes. We question whether highlighting these issues actually promotes the successful investigation, prosecution, and disruption of wildlife trafficking perpetrators and their criminal networks.
Thank you.
