Ambassador Dorothy Shea
Acting U.S. Representative
New York, New York
June 26, 2025
AS DELIVERED
Hello. I would like to thank Ambassador Yamazaki Kazuyuki, Minister Hayashi Yoshimasa, and the Japanese government for leading today’s symposium, which helps ensure continued international attention to the abduction of individuals from their loved ones. The United States is proud to co-sponsor this event.
Thank you to the family members here with us today for bravely speaking out on this issue. The United States supports the families of the victims. That includes those abducted by North Korea – not only from the Republic of Korea and Japan, but also from every nation affected.
President Trump met personally with abductee family members twice during his first term and he continues to watch this issue in hopes of a resolution.
Abductees like Yokota Megumi, a Japanese girl abducted in 1977 at the age of 13 while walking home from school. And Taguchi Yaeko, a 22-year-old mother abducted in 1978.
For what reasons were this girl and this young woman taken at such young ages? Their families still do not know what happened or why. They deserve answers.
Meaningful resolution has remained elusive for decades. As time passes, family members continue to age and, tragically, some pass away without ever learning the fate of their loved ones.
We call for the immediate resolution of these cases to reunite aging family members and provide long-overdue answers about the fate of their loved ones.
The United States will continue to prioritize this issue until every family receives the information they deserve.
To the families, your unwavering advocacy honors the memory of your loved ones and keeps hope alive. Your courage in the face of uncertainty inspires us all.
The United States stands firmly with you. You are not alone in this fight. Our hearts and prayers are with you.
