Political Advisor
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mister President, for convening today’s discussion.
The United States will continue to consider appropriate applications of the framework authorized by UN Security Council Resolution 2719.
Threats to peace and security in Africa demand innovative solutions, backed by sustainable and predictable financing.
We encourage the AU and UN 2719 Task Teams to accelerate efforts to complete the joint roadmap for the implementation of Resolution 2719.
As these preparations continue, we support ongoing efforts to proactively plan for the first application of the 2719 framework.
As the first mission will serve as a proof of concept that establishes this new tool’s value, we look forward to reviewing potential missions on a case-by-case basis and on the merits of each particular proposal. We will not support proposals that risk undermining the longevity of the tool or that contradict the spirit and word of the framework.
We encourage Member States of the AU and the UN to step up, and for the UN and the AU to consider new donors, partnerships, and funding models as a means of mobilizing the unfunded 25 percent of the total annual budget costs of any UN Security Council-authorized 2719 mission.
Any proposal for a 2719 mission should come with a clear plan for mobilizing costs not funded by assessed contributions. We also encourage the AU to demonstrate strong African ownership of peace and security solutions through contributions from the Peace Fund, whether from the principal or interest.
We recall that the UN has the responsibility to ensure oversight and accountability of Member State assessed contributions in line with the approved regulations and rules of the UN General Assembly.
We support increased accountability and transparency in UN peacekeeping as well as AU-led Peace Support Operations.
Just like UN missions, AU-led missions require clear, sequenced and prioritized mandates to ensure objectives remain focused and achievable. Success should be evaluated using evidence-based indicators. Clear exit strategies and viable transition plans must be in place to prevent mission creep and prolonging failed approaches.
Through these steps, we encourage the AU and UN to continue working toward fulfilling the promise of Security Council Resolution 2719 to promote more effective and sustainable African-led solutions to conflict.
Thank you.
