Ambassador Dorothy Shea
Acting U.S. Representative
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Madam President. And I would also like to thank Special Representative Viriginia Gamba and Director Sheema Sen Gupta for your briefings. And I want to thank Sila for sharing her compelling words with us.
This year’s annual CAAC report provides a sobering snapshot of the devastating impact of armed conflict on children.
In 2024, the United Nations reported the highest number of violations since the establishment of the monitoring and reporting mechanism.
This includes a dramatic increase in the number of cases of sexual violence.
This stark reality serves as a poignant reminder of the urgency and necessity of strengthening the international community’s child protection capacities, and of the crucial need of all parties to a conflict to respect their international obligations.
As a result of escalating gang violence, Haiti was reported as one of the countries with the greatest number of violations and abuses against children.
A large majority of these violations were committed by the Viv Ansanm coalition.
We continue to condemn Viv Ansanm’s actions and support their first-time inclusion in the annex of the Secretary-General’s report.
In May of this year, the United States designated Viv Ansanm as a Foreign Terrorist Organization.
We will continue to support efforts to promote accountability, and we intend to nominate them as well in the UN Haiti Sanctions regime.
In Sudan, growing instability threatens the safety and well-being of children.
Even with the Council’s extension of the Sudan sanctions regime earlier this year, further steps must be taken to resolve restrictions on humanitarian aid, and persistent human rights violations, and mass displacement.
Several colleagues have raised the tragic situation faced by children in Gaza.
It is important to remember that Israel has the right to defend itself and has taken numerous measures to limit harm to civilians and address humanitarian needs.
While the loss of civilian life in Gaza is tragic, the responsibility for this conflict rests with Hamas, which could stop the fighting today by freeing the hostages and agreeing to the ceasefire terms already accepted by Israel.
On October 7, 2023, Hamas brutally massacred 1,200 people, including 40 children.
Among them were siblings Ariel and Kfir Bibas — then only 4 years and 9 months old, respectively.
Hamas murdered the Bibas children and then paraded their coffins through the streets.
This terrorist organization continues to use civilians, including children, as human shields and refuses to accept a ceasefire that would bring calm to Gaza.
At the same time, Hamas aims to prevent delivery of lifesaving aid, murdering eight innocent Palestinians working on behalf of the Gaza Humanitarian Foundation on June 11.
We strongly condemn Hamas’ actions and regret that the report does not highlight the full scale of their abuses.
In this fourth year of the full-scale invasion of Ukraine and the continued occupation by Russian forces, we elevate the plight of children caught in the crossfire.
Russian armed forces and affiliated groups continue to kill and maim children, with many facing injuries or death from explosive weapons.
We call on both sides to negotiate an end to this war, and safeguard humanitarian access in all occupied areas.
We also acknowledge the continued search for the remaining children forcibly transferred or deported by Russian armed forces and the Russian authorities identified by the Secretary-General’s report last year.
They must be reunited with their families and safely returned to Ukrainian controlled territory.
The children of the Democratic Republic of the Congo continue to face mounting violence and instability as well.
We call on the DRC and Rwandan governments to revitalize efforts to reduce violations against children, including unlawful child recruitment, abduction, and all other violence against children.
We encourage all parties to the conflict in the DRC to prioritize the well-being and safety of children.
Madam President, there are some bright spots.
We commend the Somali National Army and Somali Police Force for their implementation of an action plan and their clear improvements on protecting children, as described in the report.
Likewise, we commend Iraq, Pakistan, and the Philippines for their complete delisting from the Secretary-General’s report.
We continue to encourage entities named in the report to work on action plans with the SRSG’s office and to work towards delisting.
Colleagues, children around the world deserve to feel safe, to receive an education, to have a future.
When we take steps to protect children, we are safeguarding our collective future and helping to end enduring conflicts.
We must do better.
I thank you.
