Thank you, Ambassador Leendertse. We appreciate your devoting to chairing the Peacebuilding Commission. We are also grateful for the remarks of the German Minister of State, the Former President of Liberia, the esteemed Foreign Ministers of the Gambia and Timor Leste.
We also appreciate the efforts of the distinguished Permanent Representatives of Egypt and Slovenia as co-facilitators of this year’s Peacebuilding Architecture Review (PBAR), and thank them for circulating an initial draft earlier this week.
Colleagues, conflict prevention, diplomacy, and peacebuilding are critical to addressing shared security and development challenges worldwide.
International donors alone cannot prevent conflict. Without the buy-in of parties to the conflict, UN peacebuilding will not be as effective as we all wish it to be.
Over the years, we have witnessed the impact that modest investments can have in supporting post-conflict environments, including in preventing relapse into conflict.
However, continued and often prolonged conflicts continue to beleaguer well intentioned peacebuilding efforts.
We commend the Commission for efforts in guiding responses across the peace continuum, including the implementation of peace agreements, political dialogue, promotion of coexistence, peaceful conflict resolution, revitalization of economic growth, and re-establishment of essential administrative services.
In order to be effective, UN peacebuilding must ensure outcomes that bring meaningful and material conflict prevention and lasting peace in meeting the needs of its many stakeholders.
Specifically, this year’s Peacebuilding Architecture Review presents an opportunity to improve our approach to UN peacebuilding through enhanced coherence on implementation within the UN system, strengthened cooperation with international financial institutions, and improved integration of human rights.
Additionally, the Peacebuilding Architecture Review must continue to center national ownership in conflict prevention and peacebuilding efforts, including through national prevention strategies.
In closing, we urge all members to take their commitments to ending conflicts seriously, and to take concrete steps that advance the cause of international peace.
We look forward to forthcoming discussions that could inform future peacebuilding efforts at the UN.
Thank you.
