Thank you, Mr. President, Minister Hugh Todd, for convening today’s important discussion. And thank you also to Secretary-General Antonio Guterres, Assistant Secretary-General Kanni Wignaraja, and AU Commission Chairperson Mahmoud Ali Youssouf for your remarks.
160207
Peace is not merely the absence of conflict; it is the presence of security and opportunity for all.
It is the foundation upon which societies can thrive, economies can grow, and human potential can be fully realized.
Peace is the essential bedrock upon which we must build our collective futures.
This is why the United States supports this Council’s return to its founding mission of maintaining peace and security around the world.
Mr. President, at the same time, national ownership in both conflict prevention and peacebuilding efforts remain critical to lasting peace.
The UN and other international actors alone cannot prevent conflict nor build peace.
It is essential that national governments commit to peace and demonstrate that they undertake these commitments seriously.
Addressing the causes of conflict is essential for achieving peace and stability.
By tackling these underlying factors, national governments can create an environment conducive to long-term prosperity for their citizens.
Mr. President, when using peacekeeping and peacebuilding missions as tools to prevent reoccurrence of conflict, the UN must engage in integrated planning at both mission and headquarters levels.
Peacekeeping missions, for example, need clear end states, measurable goals, and a streamlined support structure to allocate resources responsibly.
Evidence-based indicators are essential to demonstrate the impact of peacekeeping missions.
As we strive to advance the cause of peace for all humans, depart from the era of barbarism of the past, and restore this body’s efforts to securing lasting peace, we call upon members to take their commitments seriously.
The United States is committed to working with our partners in the Security Council, regional organizations, and across the UN system to enhance our collective efforts to prevent conflict and build peace to ensure a safer and more prosperous world for all.
Thank you, Mr. President.
