Jonathan Shrier
Acting ECOSOC Representative
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President.
Safety is a precondition to all of us exercising our freedom, and justice is secured only when those who break the law face real consequences.
I know that policy ideas resulting from this debate, which are in turn submitted to the Commission on Crime Prevention and Criminal Justice, may address prison overcrowding and poor facility or inmate conditions through non-custodial alternatives to incarceration.
But the United States calls upon States to acknowledge that these approaches can result in a deterioration of public safety when offenders are released who then engage in other wrongdoing. Punishment and deterrence – not just rehabilitation – are legitimate rationales for criminal justice measures. We can’t forget that.
As States discuss ideas to address poor prison conditions, sure, let’s emphasize effective prison management and case management. Let’s discuss ways to improve the efficiency and effectiveness of criminal justice proceedings.
And let’s remember that, when utilized properly, swift delivery of due process for the accused ensures that certain non-violent offenders aren’t subjected to costly, extended pre-trial detention that drains the public fisc.
These are worthy topics for discussion. But ultimately the Nelson Mandela Rules we are commemorating, as well as the Bangkok Rules and Tokyo Rules, are non-binding and do not infringe on the sovereignty of States.
And international conferences like this one should never lose sight of the true guarantors of public safety – the hardworking men and women, who, every day, wake up and grant or deny bail. Who feed and manage inmates. Who try to steer individuals prone to criminal behavior toward a better way.
Those guardians aren’t here. But it is them – not us – who work under dangerous conditions. It is more them – and less us – who preserve, through their actions and decisions, the stable, secure societies in which we all hope to live.
Mr. President, because time is short, I will move on to mention one final, separate note from the United States: the use of the word “gender” in the Mandela Rules, Bangkok Rules, and Tokyo Rules refers to women and girls and men and boys. The United States rejects any potential attempts to corrupt these clear guidelines with activist gender ideology that denies biological reality.
Further, the United States emphasizes our immovable objections to the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and the Sustainable Development Goals. This framework advances a program of soft global governance and distract from fundamental work on criminal justice.
In closing, Mr. President, we reiterate our demand that States respect all of the God-given, unalienable rights of U.S. citizens confined in prisons abroad. As President Trump has made clear, U.S. citizens wrongfully detained abroad must be released.
We look forward to working with all states to prevent crime and advance justice by holding criminals accountable.
Thank you.
