Ambassador Dorothy Shea
Acting U.S. Representative
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, President Yang.
Today’s Emergency Special Session marks yet another failure on the part of the United Nations to condemn Hamas.
We remain incensed that just yesterday, Hamas murdered five Palestinian civilians working to distribute aid for the Gaza Humanitarian Foundation.
The resolution being considered does nothing to free the hostages, improve the lives of civilians in Gaza, or bring us closer to a ceasefire, and is yet another performative action that erodes the credibility of this body.
This draft resolution sends an unacceptable message to Hamas and other Iran-backed terrorist proxies, and that message is: you will be rewarded for taking hostages, diverting aid, and launching attacks from civilian areas.
Mr. President, colleagues, blind support to Hamas by the members of this body further undermines the meaningful diplomacy underway to free the hostages and emboldens Hamas as the terrorist group continues to reject ceasefire proposals accepted by Israel.
As the United States has made clear time and time again, we do not support one-sided measures that fail to condemn Hamas.
We will not support resolutions that do not call for violent terrorist groups to disarm and leave Gaza and fail to recognize Israel’s right to defend itself.
It would be a better use of time for this body to support the diplomatic efforts currently underway to free the remaining 53 hostages, many of whom are nationals of member states of this body, and remain in deplorable conditions for the last 615 days.
Instead, the UN is wasting valuable time, energy, and resources on yet another biased resolution that rewards Hamas.
Let us be clear. This resolution does nothing to bring calm to Gaza. It also does nothing to advance a realistic diplomatic solution to further the cause of peace. It is rife with serious defects.
This resolution fails to acknowledge Israel has the right to defend itself. As we have made clear, any measure that undercuts our ally’s security is a non-starter for the United States.
This resolution draws false equivalence between Israel and Hamas, which is not only morally reprehensible, but also naive and dangerous.
This resolution seeks an immediate, unconditional, and permanent ceasefire that would leave Hamas in a position to carry out future October 7th attacks.
Mr. President, no one wants to see Palestinian civilians in Gaza go hungry or thirsty, yet this resolution fails to acknowledge the shortcomings of the prior method of aid delivery, which allowed Hamas to enrich itself at the expense of the Palestinian civilians it purports to represent.
We reiterate our call for the UN, NGOs, and partners to support the Gaza Humanitarian Foundation to help it safely deliver aid without allowing Hamas to pilfer those resources.
Hamas continues to weaponize the suffering of civilians in Gaza, murdering Palestinians as the just did yesterday, and injuring many others, in another desperate bid to obstruct the delivery of aid and maintain control.
This resolution falsely accuses Israel of the use of starvation as a method of warfare, while at the same time ignoring Gaza Humanitarian Foundation efforts to cut out Hamas and deliver aid consistent with the humanitarian principles of humanity, neutrality, impartiality, and independence.
This resolution callously demands that Israel end its blockade on Gaza while conveniently ignoring the fact that Hamas, which committed the worst massacre of Jews since the Holocaust and abducted 251 people on October 7, 2023, continues to pose an active threat to Israeli civilians and to brutally suppress protests against its own despotic rule.
It also fails to recognize the extensive measures undertaken by Israel to mitigate harm to civilians in Gaza, even as Hamas continues to use noncombatants as human shields.
Mr. President, the United States will support a future for the people in Gaza that is free of Hamas and full of opportunity. We will do our part to help forge this new reality alongside Israel and our Arab partners.
A durable peace between Israelis and Palestinians, and a more prosperous future for all of the region’s people will only be possible through direct negotiations between the parties – and not unilaterally imposed from afar.
We urge fellow members to call out Hamas for what is the biggest impediment toward a life of prosperity for people living in Gaza.
We urge members to vote against this biased resolution, which will further enflame tensions at a time when negotiators are continuing their efforts to secure a durable ceasefire in Gaza.
Thank you.
