Jacob McGee
DRL Deputy Assistant Secretary
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
The United States extends its gratitude to Ms. Karla Quintana and the IIMP for their critical work to advance the search for the missing in Syria, including American citizens kidnapped by ISIS and the Assad regime.
We are encouraged by your visit to Syria and engagement with the Syrian government, families, and civil society.
Nearly every Syrian family is personally impacted by the widespread and systemic use of detention, torture, and killing under the Assad regime. Families of those missing deserve answers.
The United States stands with the Syrian people at this extraordinary historic moment, and champions Syria’s efforts to establish a peaceful and prosperous country.
We welcome the establishment of the national commissions on transitional justice and missing persons, which are initial steps towards truth and justice.
We call on the Syrian government to ensure these commissions address abuses by all parties and are independent, transparent, and inclusive of victims, survivors, and families.
We also encourage close cooperation with the IIMP (Double-Eye-M-P) to help families get long-deserved answers, including for Americans and partner country nationals.
Broad and enduring stability in Syria will depend on clarifying the fate and whereabouts of more than 125,000 missing persons and advancing meaningful justice and accountability for abuses by all parties over the past 14 years.
As Secretary Rubio said, a peaceful, stable Syria would be one of the most dramatic improvements in the region in a very long time, and we want to do everything we can to help achieve that.
