Jonathan Shrier
Acting Representative of the United States on the ECOSOC
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President and thank you, Dr. Kanem for your statement. We wish you the best in your next endeavors.
The United States recognizes the importance of advancing the health and wellbeing of women and girls. Improving maternal health, preventing obstetric fistula, and reducing maternal mortality are essential so that women everywhere can experience safe pregnancy and childbirth and the joys of being a mother.
Similarly, violence against women and girls around the world must end, and this includes in humanitarian settings. We abominate the prevalence of female genital mutilation and cutting as well as child, early, and forced marriage and call on all actors to intensify their efforts to eliminate these forms of violence. The next Strategic Plan must focus on these core mandate issues. Through the Geneva Consensus Declaration, the United States pursues these shared objectives for improved health for women and girls.
For decades, the United States has called on UNFPA to end its partnership with the Chinese government agency responsible for implementing coercive policies. Millions of families suffered under China’s abhorrent population control. Generations of women were subject to barbaric forced abortions and involuntary sterilization and continue to suffer from the harm of this excruciating legacy. Where is their justice? UNFPA’s silence on the health and well-being of those women suffering still today is deafening. UNFPA should not be complicit in China’s population control. Once again, the United States has determined the Kemp-Kasten Amendment precludes the United States from providing funding to UNFPA.
Furthermore, we urge the Executive Board to ask why UNFPA still subsidizes technical assistance for a country with the second largest economy in the world. Given the funding environment, how can UNFPA justify the allocation of precious resources every year to programming in China? We call on the Board to exercise its fiduciary responsibility in relation to oversight and accountability particularly in relation to UNFPA’s work in China.
Finally, the United States must urge UNFPA to remove divisive cultural concepts from its programming, especially diversity, equity, and inclusion; gender ideology and sexual and reproductive health. The United States expects the United Nations to avoid using diversity, equity, and inclusion concepts and terminology. The United Nations should align with that policy by using terminology that clearly refers to women and men, girls and boys, and avoids ambiguous terms.
Thank you.
